# Free-Tier Capacity Analysis

This document outlines the maximum theoretical load the Stitch-Opt B2B SaaS platform can sustain while running strictly on $0/month free-tier infrastructure (Render Free, Supabase Free, Cloudinary Free, Groq API Free).

## 1. System Stability vs Daily Load

The system is mathematically projected to remain perfectly stable up to **3,000 requests per day**. Beyond 4,000 requests, peak-hour traffic spikes will begin triggering API rate limits and memory exhaustion.

```mermaid
xychart-beta
    title "System Stability vs Daily Requests (Free Tier)"
    x-axis "Requests per Day" ["1,000", "2,000", "3,000", "4,000", "5,000", "6,000"]
    y-axis "Stability Percentage (%)" 0 --> 100
    bar [100, 100, 95, 70, 35, 5]
```

### The "Danger Zone" (4,000 - 5,000 Requests)
At 5,000 daily requests, the average load is safe, but **lunch-time or weekend traffic rushes** will easily push the server to 25+ requests per minute. At this threshold, Groq's free-tier AI API will enforce a `429 Too Many Requests` hard block, temporarily disabling the chatbot and automated workflows for all users.

---

## 2. The Render RAM Bottleneck

The absolute weakest link in the free stack is the **512MB RAM Limit** on Render. The base Node.js server idles around `100MB`. The `@xenova/transformers` library (which generates mathematical vector embeddings for the AI Search) consumes roughly `250MB` of RAM when active.

If multiple users trigger an AI embedding at the exact same second, the RAM requirements compound.

```mermaid
xychart-beta
    title "Render RAM Consumption vs Concurrent AI Math Spikes"
    x-axis "Simultaneous AI Requests" ["1 User", "2 Users", "3 Users", "4 Users", "5 Users", "6+ Users"]
    y-axis "RAM Usage (MB)" 0 --> 600
    bar [350, 390, 430, 470, 510, 550]
```

> [!CAUTION]
> **Out of Memory (OOM) Crashes**
> As shown in the chart above, if **5 to 6 customers** ask the AI a question at the exact same millisecond, RAM usage exceeds 512MB. Render will instantly kill the server process to prevent hardware damage, resulting in a temporary 502 Bad Gateway error for everyone.

